{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.markdown_literals`. I found no gaps.\n\n**Escaped roles stay literal**\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal]` includes `\\{role}` in files with extensions `.md`, `.markdown`, `.MD` and `.Markdown`. It asserts that no reference is collected from them. A `.txt` copy of the same text is still collected, which shows that the Markdown literal rule applies only to Markdown files.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal]` checks the escape in the book preprocessor. It also checks that `\\\\{role}` (an escaped backslash) does not escape the role, and that `\\\\\\{role}` does.\n\n**Inline code and fenced code stay literal**\n- The same two tests cover inline code and fenced code.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[citation_metadata_preserves_prose_other_roles_and_literal_contexts]` adds `~~~` fences and fenced directives (`:::{cite}` and `:::{note}`) inside code fences.\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_fences_close_with_enough_colons_and_skip_code]` covers a `:::{cite}` fence inside a code block, but it is not marked as a test of this requirement.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown]` checks that a role in a fenced block inside a `{req}` body is ignored.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs]` checks the same for notes files.\n\n**Raw HTML stays literal**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers an HTML block (`<div>`), an HTML comment (both on its own and inline), and an attribute value in an inline tag. It asserts that exactly one reference remains, the role between `<em>` tags. It also asserts that the rendered book output keeps the literals and badges only the role between the tags, and that plain text gets no HTML protection.\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_fences_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers colon-fence references inside an HTML block, a comment and an inline tag attribute. It asserts that only the fence outside HTML counts.\n- The `every_markdown_extension…` test and the citation test also cover an attribute value and a `<div>`, a comment and a `<span>`.\n\n**Term roles**\n- `src/terms.rs#mod[tests]/fn[term_dependencies_of_every_markdown_extension_skip_literal_roles]` and `tests/definitions.rs#fn[literal_term_roles_in_subject_and_requirements_inputs_need_no_definition]` apply the same literal contexts to `{term}` roles. The second test also checks that the same role written as a real reference does produce an error.\n\n**A `{req}` directive in a fenced example does not define a requirement**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal]` puts an indented `:::{req} block` inside a code fence. It asserts that the text stays literal, while a real `:::{req} visible` directive does render as a requirement.\n- `definition_blocks` is the shared definition finder, used by the book renderer and by the evidence loader (`collect_definitions`). The test of the renderer therefore exercises the same logic the loader uses.\n\n**Legacy inline `{req}` role does not define a requirement**\n- `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` and `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal]` assert that `{req}`legacy`` stays literal. The second also asserts that no `habeas-req-requirements.inline` anchor is produced.\n\nEach distinct context the text names is exercised with assertions on the expected outcome, and the tests are marked `{test}` to this requirement.",
  "references": "ca60d4670142470efe95739613ce4bd954b811884e20ae77ca354078db23fb16",
